WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:10.130
- So, welcome and thank you everyone

00:00:10.130 --> 00:00:12.010
for joining us this morning.

00:00:12.010 --> 00:00:13.370
My name is Reilly Hartigan.

00:00:13.370 --> 00:00:15.420
I'm joined today by my colleagues,

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:17.680
Meredith Crawford and Laurie Toth,

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:20.830
and we're here to talk a little bit about

00:00:20.830 --> 00:00:22.510
JIRA and Confluence

00:00:22.510 --> 00:00:24.960
and how at the Yale School of Management,

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:27.760
we use that beyond IT, and for things

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:29.050
that are not technical.

00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:32.930
So non-traditional uses
of JIRA and Confluence.

00:00:32.930 --> 00:00:35.160
Perfect, much better.

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:36.740
So, before we begin,

00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:39.410
I just wanna kind of gauge
who is in the audience.

00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:43.310
So, if you have used JIRA
or Confluence before,

00:00:43.310 --> 00:00:45.330
go ahead and raise your
hand...shout at me.

00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:49.060
Okay! So, perfect.

00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:51.030
Um, how about the other questions?

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:52.210
Do any of these apply to you?

00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:54.030
You've, you know, haven't been able

00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:55.730
to find an email when you've needed

00:00:55.730 --> 00:00:57.840
to get some information.

00:00:57.840 --> 00:00:59.810
Um, you feel like you have an ever-growing

00:00:59.810 --> 00:01:01.030
to-do list.

00:01:01.030 --> 00:01:03.330
And finally, since we're in New Haven,

00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:04.983
how many of you like pizza?

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:08.960
Yes! Okay awesome, awesome.

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:10.920
Round of applause for New Haven Pizza.

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:12.290
So pizza comes in later.

00:01:12.290 --> 00:01:15.370
I have a really
embarrassing thing to admit

00:01:15.370 --> 00:01:16.203
but, uh

00:01:16.203 --> 00:01:17.300
so if you're not awake right now,

00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:20.050
you might be in about three minutes.

00:01:20.050 --> 00:01:22.670
So, what we're gonna cover today

00:01:22.670 --> 00:01:24.970
congratulations, you're
in the right session,

00:01:24.970 --> 00:01:26.780
we are gonna cover first,

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:28.560
I'll take on what exactly these

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:31.051
tools are, then I will hand over

00:01:31.051 --> 00:01:32.940
the presentation to my colleagues

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:34.400
Meredith and Laurie.

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:36.150
We'll talk a bit about why we use

00:01:36.150 --> 00:01:38.560
these tools, and how we use them

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:40.850
day-to-day at the Yale
School of Management.

00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:42.760
And finally, I'll be joining you again

00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:45.290
to give some advice if any of this

00:01:45.290 --> 00:01:47.142
um, you know, inspires you

00:01:47.142 --> 00:01:49.610
you wanna go ahead and give this a shot,

00:01:49.610 --> 00:01:51.470
give you some advice and tips

00:01:51.470 --> 00:01:52.950
from what we learned.

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:55.630
So, without further ado, let's dig in.

00:01:55.630 --> 00:01:57.500
Literally, pizza.

00:01:57.500 --> 00:01:59.370
Um, and this is where the funny story

00:01:59.370 --> 00:02:00.203
comes in.

00:02:00.203 --> 00:02:01.380
So a lot of people are probably familiar

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:04.850
with the pizza analogy, um...

00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:07.740
to describe workflows and we use

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:10.000
this a lot, people use this a lot

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:10.990
so I'm gonna use it again.

00:02:10.990 --> 00:02:13.320
So bear with me if you've heard it before.

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:14.770
Um, but we're gonna imagine that

00:02:14.770 --> 00:02:17.510
we're all running a pizzeria, okay?

00:02:17.510 --> 00:02:18.930
And we have to do a certain amount of

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:21.270
things to get a pizza, from someone

00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:22.910
ordering it, to getting that pizza out

00:02:22.910 --> 00:02:24.480
the door, right?

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:27.270
So, we're gonna build
a basic pizza tracker.

00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:31.003
And that's just going to consist of:

00:02:32.470 --> 00:02:34.850
Someone places an order, it's prepped

00:02:34.850 --> 00:02:36.500
so, there's a customer

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:38.290
one of you places in an order,

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:40.180
one of you is putting the toppings

00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:41.340
on the pizza.

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:43.160
One of you puts it in the oven,

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:44.353
the other checks that it's good,

00:02:44.353 --> 00:02:45.780
puts it in the box.

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:48.120
Someone delivers it, and someone's

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:50.320
lucky enough to go eat that pizza.

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:51.330
And now that we have this

00:02:51.330 --> 00:02:53.103
beautiful pizza tracker, we can see

00:02:53.103 --> 00:02:54.547
that we're doing pretty well in our

00:02:54.547 --> 00:02:55.710
pizzeria, right?

00:02:55.710 --> 00:02:58.260
We can see we're claiming a lot of orders,

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:01.040
so, some people are really picky.

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:03.903
Do the half of this, not with that.

00:03:03.903 --> 00:03:05.490
Wish it could be simple.

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:07.440
And as things are going on,

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:09.140
we see an issue.

00:03:09.140 --> 00:03:10.970
And we're able to
(background noise) because

00:03:10.970 --> 00:03:13.530
we built this, pizza tracker

00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:15.517
and here we have our customers saying,

00:03:15.517 --> 00:03:17.330
"Yo, where's my pizza?"

00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:19.197
and you have the manager saying,

00:03:19.197 --> 00:03:21.000
"What's going on?"

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:22.650
And thankfully, because we built

00:03:22.650 --> 00:03:25.040
this pizza tracker,

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:27.700
we can see maybe where the issue is.

00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:30.090
So, there are no pizzas in the oven.

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:31.890
It's probably a problem.

00:03:31.890 --> 00:03:33.550
Because it means we have no pizzas in

00:03:33.550 --> 00:03:35.820
boxes, which means we have no pizzas for

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:37.520
our really sad customer over here.

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:39.650
And now comes for the real,

00:03:39.650 --> 00:03:41.044
really embarrassing part that I

00:03:41.044 --> 00:03:42.568
mentioned earlier?

00:03:42.568 --> 00:03:44.297
I wasn't (mumbles) and I was like

00:03:44.297 --> 00:03:46.340
let me use the pizza analogy to

00:03:46.340 --> 00:03:48.949
describe a basic workflow because

00:03:48.949 --> 00:03:51.320
we're in New Haven (background noise).

00:03:51.320 --> 00:03:52.557
So my hand shot up and I said,

00:03:52.557 --> 00:03:54.807
"It's just like the
Domino's Pizza Tracker!",

00:03:56.120 --> 00:03:58.580
which immediately I was laughed at.

00:03:58.580 --> 00:04:00.510
So, I've been vegan for four years

00:04:00.510 --> 00:04:02.813
and I still sometimes order Domino's.

00:04:04.090 --> 00:04:08.670
So, if you recognize that pizza workflow,

00:04:08.670 --> 00:04:11.320
it looks a lot like this.

00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:13.750
So, keep that in mind,

00:04:13.750 --> 00:04:15.170
we'll come back later.

00:04:15.170 --> 00:04:17.560
But basically, to come full circle.

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:19.703
I'm, uh, yeah.

00:04:25.065 --> 00:04:27.403
I think the batteries have died?

00:04:27.403 --> 00:04:30.099
- [Tech] Now you're on.

00:04:30.099 --> 00:04:32.432
- (mumbles)

00:04:33.754 --> 00:04:37.671
That's okay though, (mumbles).

00:04:41.555 --> 00:04:43.124
It was green, now it's--

00:04:43.124 --> 00:04:44.291
No, it's okay.

00:04:48.178 --> 00:04:49.011
It's okay.

00:04:50.036 --> 00:04:51.550
No, no worries.

00:04:51.550 --> 00:04:52.717
So, apologies.

00:04:56.350 --> 00:04:58.604
I promise this was not a ploy for me

00:04:58.604 --> 00:05:02.673
to distract you with
extra time not at all.

00:05:06.770 --> 00:05:09.380
Can you hear me now? Perfect.

00:05:09.380 --> 00:05:11.740
All right, so, to come full circle

00:05:11.740 --> 00:05:13.460
what are JIRA and Confluence?

00:05:13.460 --> 00:05:15.610
JIRA is basically a pizza tracker.

00:05:15.610 --> 00:05:18.812
So, we can see a basic workflow in JIRA

00:05:18.812 --> 00:05:21.650
To Do, In Progress, and Done

00:05:21.650 --> 00:05:23.860
and we just built

00:05:23.860 --> 00:05:25.030
a custom workflow for

00:05:25.030 --> 00:05:26.760
non-traditional use of JIRA

00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:28.220
with our pizza tracker.

00:05:28.220 --> 00:05:29.970
We have Order Placed,

00:05:29.970 --> 00:05:31.470
our In Progress statuses with

00:05:31.470 --> 00:05:34.120
Prep, Baking, Box, Out For Delivery,

00:05:34.120 --> 00:05:36.250
and then finally a resolution of Done.

00:05:36.250 --> 00:05:37.740
And, I'm using some of these words

00:05:37.740 --> 00:05:38.960
because, again, a lot of you are

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:40.903
familiar with JIRA and Confluence.

00:05:41.980 --> 00:05:45.010
So now that we've gone through that,

00:05:45.010 --> 00:05:47.630
we can finally come full circle

00:05:47.630 --> 00:05:50.060
and explain the analogy.

00:05:50.060 --> 00:05:53.530
So, in this story of our pizzeria,

00:05:53.530 --> 00:05:55.290
JIRA is really the pizza tracker.

00:05:55.290 --> 00:05:57.170
It's tracking those items along

00:05:57.170 --> 00:05:58.610
that workflow.

00:05:58.610 --> 00:06:00.660
JIRA Service Desk is kind of,

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:02.400
embarrassingly, again, like that

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:03.950
Domino's Pizza Tracker.

00:06:03.950 --> 00:06:06.160
It's really where the customer is seeing

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:07.550
you know, where's my pizza?

00:06:07.550 --> 00:06:09.130
How long is it gonna take to

00:06:09.130 --> 00:06:09.963
get to me?

00:06:09.963 --> 00:06:12.270
What's the status of that pizza?

00:06:12.270 --> 00:06:15.180
And finally, Confluence is a place

00:06:15.180 --> 00:06:16.890
where we can all collaborate,

00:06:16.890 --> 00:06:19.410
write pizza recipes, write guidelines

00:06:19.410 --> 00:06:22.180
about how we work, and finally

00:06:22.180 --> 00:06:25.168
a pizza can be anything that you'd like

00:06:25.168 --> 00:06:26.720
in JIRA.

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:28.810
So to talk about those anythings,

00:06:28.810 --> 00:06:30.060
in JIRA, I'm gonna

00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:32.840
hand it over now to my colleague

00:06:32.840 --> 00:06:33.830
Meredith, who's gonna talk

00:06:33.830 --> 00:06:36.640
a little bit about what those things are

00:06:36.640 --> 00:06:38.290
at the Yale School of Management.

00:06:40.370 --> 00:06:41.430
- Thanks, Reilly.

00:06:41.430 --> 00:06:43.320
Hi. I'm Meredith Crawford.

00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:46.850
I handle editorial workflow for the

00:06:46.850 --> 00:06:49.400
Communications Department at SoM,

00:06:49.400 --> 00:06:50.233
and, uh

00:06:51.513 --> 00:06:53.290
advance a slide here,

00:06:53.290 --> 00:06:55.340
some of you might recognize this

00:06:55.340 --> 00:06:58.563
as a basic, screenshot of Basecamp.

00:06:59.660 --> 00:07:01.870
Before we found JIRA and Confluence

00:07:01.870 --> 00:07:03.330
as a workflow solution,

00:07:03.330 --> 00:07:05.130
we were using Basecamp,

00:07:05.130 --> 00:07:06.550
which I think a lot of editorial teams

00:07:06.550 --> 00:07:07.383
probably do.

00:07:07.383 --> 00:07:08.490
And we were using it to manage

00:07:08.490 --> 00:07:10.240
our communications projects,

00:07:10.240 --> 00:07:11.500
which at any given time,

00:07:11.500 --> 00:07:13.680
we probably have dozens of,

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:14.970
and they range in complexity.

00:07:14.970 --> 00:07:16.790
So some of them are
really straightforward.

00:07:16.790 --> 00:07:18.450
They don't require a lot of

00:07:18.450 --> 00:07:20.560
complicated workflow.

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:22.130
They just go from

00:07:22.130 --> 00:07:24.810
To Do to In Progress to Done,

00:07:24.810 --> 00:07:26.160
but some of them have

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:27.410
a lot of different assets,

00:07:27.410 --> 00:07:29.773
a lot of stakeholders, a lot of creators.

00:07:30.773 --> 00:07:33.880
And also, we have a built-in processor

00:07:33.880 --> 00:07:34.713
where we need to send our

00:07:34.713 --> 00:07:38.650
content out to external stakeholders

00:07:38.650 --> 00:07:40.160
for approval.

00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:41.410
So, in addition to that,

00:07:41.410 --> 00:07:43.140
we also have a Post-Publish.

00:07:43.140 --> 00:07:44.510
So, once our work is published,

00:07:44.510 --> 00:07:46.470
we also have another workflow

00:07:46.470 --> 00:07:48.170
to consider, and that's how to

00:07:48.170 --> 00:07:50.200
promote our content on social

00:07:50.200 --> 00:07:51.960
and maybe, how to create something

00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:53.190
on the website to feature it

00:07:53.190 --> 00:07:55.290
more prominently.

00:07:55.290 --> 00:07:56.380
And when we were doing all of

00:07:56.380 --> 00:07:57.440
these things in Basecamp,

00:07:57.440 --> 00:07:58.728
we were really limited

00:07:58.728 --> 00:08:02.170
when it came to differentiating

00:08:02.170 --> 00:08:05.930
the various tasks within one project.

00:08:05.930 --> 00:08:07.110
Everything had to be under

00:08:07.110 --> 00:08:08.690
one umbrella.

00:08:08.690 --> 00:08:10.120
And so, we would end up with

00:08:10.120 --> 00:08:12.490
these huge threads that sometimes

00:08:12.490 --> 00:08:15.010
went on for months at a time.

00:08:15.010 --> 00:08:15.843
And I mean,

00:08:15.843 --> 00:08:16.870
you can see at the top of here

00:08:16.870 --> 00:08:21.780
we have, 3,580 discussions.

00:08:21.780 --> 00:08:23.520
So you'd have to mine your way

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:24.580
through that, and then

00:08:24.580 --> 00:08:26.770
when you were working on a project,

00:08:26.770 --> 00:08:28.360
say you were looking for a specific

00:08:28.360 --> 00:08:30.260
asset like a video.

00:08:30.260 --> 00:08:31.800
You would have to go through the thread,

00:08:31.800 --> 00:08:33.360
and, god forbid you pulled

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:34.970
the wrong video, not the most

00:08:34.970 --> 00:08:36.600
updated Word document.

00:08:36.600 --> 00:08:38.100
So it was really confusing,

00:08:38.100 --> 00:08:39.550
and we quickly realized that

00:08:39.550 --> 00:08:40.940
it would be great if we could

00:08:40.940 --> 00:08:42.990
find a workflow solution that

00:08:42.990 --> 00:08:45.570
gave us a 360 degree view

00:08:45.570 --> 00:08:46.780
of our projects.

00:08:46.780 --> 00:08:48.070
A place where we could keep

00:08:48.070 --> 00:08:49.830
everything under one umbrella,

00:08:49.830 --> 00:08:51.960
but also take into account that

00:08:51.960 --> 00:08:54.050
all of the various tasks and assets

00:08:54.050 --> 00:08:56.420
probably had their own sort of workflow

00:08:57.790 --> 00:08:59.530
to take into account.

00:08:59.530 --> 00:09:01.380
So this is where

00:09:01.380 --> 00:09:03.280
JIRA came into play.

00:09:03.280 --> 00:09:04.540
And this is just the

00:09:04.540 --> 00:09:07.180
very, very bare bones basic

00:09:07.180 --> 00:09:09.280
sort of baked in JIRA.

00:09:09.280 --> 00:09:11.830
Workflow that everybody has access to,

00:09:11.830 --> 00:09:12.663
really.

00:09:12.663 --> 00:09:14.300
And that is just, you know, gives you

00:09:14.300 --> 00:09:16.140
a very simplified workflow

00:09:16.140 --> 00:09:18.213
To Do, In Progress, and Done.

00:09:19.110 --> 00:09:21.300
So, we started with this, and

00:09:21.300 --> 00:09:23.180
we realized pretty quickly that

00:09:23.180 --> 00:09:25.650
we had almost limitless options

00:09:25.650 --> 00:09:30.090
in terms of customizing
our workflow process

00:09:30.090 --> 00:09:32.790
for all of our needs, which is great,

00:09:32.790 --> 00:09:34.410
but it's also a little daunting.

00:09:34.410 --> 00:09:37.160
So, we spent a lot of time really

00:09:37.160 --> 00:09:39.609
dialing in and trying to figure out

00:09:39.609 --> 00:09:42.890
what we needed, what we didn't need.

00:09:42.890 --> 00:09:45.600
And eventually for the editorial side,

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:48.340
we came up with this, and

00:09:48.340 --> 00:09:49.740
you'll see it still includes some

00:09:49.740 --> 00:09:53.240
of the very basic statuses that

00:09:53.240 --> 00:09:55.410
you would get in a
simplified workflow like,

00:09:55.410 --> 00:09:58.730
Open, the project hasn't been started

00:09:58.730 --> 00:10:01.810
Assigned, it's in Draft mode.

00:10:01.810 --> 00:10:04.620
We have, when we're working on something

00:10:04.620 --> 00:10:06.020
we need it to be sent out

00:10:06.020 --> 00:10:08.250
for external approval to a stakeholder,

00:10:08.250 --> 00:10:09.890
so that's a status that we decided

00:10:09.890 --> 00:10:10.833
we needed.

00:10:11.870 --> 00:10:13.240
And then, ready to be put

00:10:13.240 --> 00:10:14.780
into the CMS,

00:10:14.780 --> 00:10:16.120
Published, and Done.

00:10:16.120 --> 00:10:18.243
So this has worked really well for us,

00:10:19.300 --> 00:10:20.840
and allows us to take into account

00:10:20.840 --> 00:10:24.650
every point that we move content through

00:10:24.650 --> 00:10:26.030
and, to bring it from

00:10:26.030 --> 00:10:28.200
creation to completion.

00:10:28.200 --> 00:10:30.435
This also allows us to include

00:10:30.435 --> 00:10:32.350
post-publishing concerns like

00:10:32.350 --> 00:10:34.023
social media promotion.

00:10:36.950 --> 00:10:38.300
And the best part is that

00:10:38.300 --> 00:10:39.980
when we create a ticket

00:10:39.980 --> 00:10:42.400
to begin working on a project,

00:10:42.400 --> 00:10:44.530
we can incorporate all the elements

00:10:44.530 --> 00:10:45.610
that go along with it.

00:10:45.610 --> 00:10:47.520
So, just by adding sub tasks,

00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:50.170
we can consider various assets, like

00:10:50.170 --> 00:10:53.110
if it has a video, if it has photos

00:10:53.110 --> 00:10:54.083
that we need,

00:10:55.336 --> 00:10:58.750
if we need to promote it on social...

00:10:58.750 --> 00:10:59.610
that kind of thing.

00:10:59.610 --> 00:11:00.510
And everything is kept in

00:11:00.510 --> 00:11:02.233
one place, which is great.

00:11:04.970 --> 00:11:06.670
And, in terms of keeping it all

00:11:06.670 --> 00:11:07.960
organized, this is probably

00:11:07.960 --> 00:11:10.640
my favorite thing about JIRA.

00:11:10.640 --> 00:11:11.740
So, this is a Kanban.

00:11:11.740 --> 00:11:13.250
I don't know how many people are

00:11:13.250 --> 00:11:14.420
familiar with that.

00:11:14.420 --> 00:11:16.180
If you're not familiar with JIRA,

00:11:16.180 --> 00:11:17.920
I like to think if it as a very

00:11:17.920 --> 00:11:19.100
non-technical person.

00:11:19.100 --> 00:11:20.140
So I like to think of it of just

00:11:20.140 --> 00:11:21.160
kind of a blackboard,

00:11:21.160 --> 00:11:23.120
where everything gets aggregated.

00:11:23.120 --> 00:11:26.720
And you can really keep it organized

00:11:26.720 --> 00:11:28.890
by creating these quick filters,

00:11:28.890 --> 00:11:31.620
which are when you label content,

00:11:31.620 --> 00:11:33.610
you can then dial in and search

00:11:33.610 --> 00:11:35.400
by the quick filter.

00:11:35.400 --> 00:11:37.640
And you can see that everything is

00:11:37.640 --> 00:11:38.990
in its own column.

00:11:38.990 --> 00:11:41.200
So we have stuff that's still

00:11:41.200 --> 00:11:43.130
waiting to be done.

00:11:43.130 --> 00:11:44.360
We have stuff that's in progress,

00:11:44.360 --> 00:11:45.510
someone's reviewing it,

00:11:45.510 --> 00:11:47.660
someone's working on writing it.

00:11:47.660 --> 00:11:48.493
We have stuff that's out

00:11:48.493 --> 00:11:49.820
for approval, we have stuff that's

00:11:49.820 --> 00:11:51.010
ready for CMS.

00:11:51.010 --> 00:11:52.030
And then we have stuff that's

00:11:52.030 --> 00:11:54.640
published, and what you don't see

00:11:54.640 --> 00:11:56.803
on that is there are also some

00:11:56.803 --> 00:12:00.350
other, customizable tasks within that

00:12:00.350 --> 00:12:01.570
that say whether or not

00:12:01.570 --> 00:12:02.840
it's ready for social, or

00:12:02.840 --> 00:12:03.880
if it has been promoted

00:12:03.880 --> 00:12:04.830
on social or not.

00:12:04.830 --> 00:12:07.160
So we really really, gotten pretty

00:12:07.160 --> 00:12:10.680
granular with that customization.

00:12:10.680 --> 00:12:11.630
My favorite thing about the

00:12:11.630 --> 00:12:13.930
quick filters is that

00:12:13.930 --> 00:12:16.450
you can click on any of them,

00:12:16.450 --> 00:12:18.600
and it will just give you

00:12:18.600 --> 00:12:19.730
the relevant content.

00:12:19.730 --> 00:12:21.635
So, I was in a meeting with

00:12:21.635 --> 00:12:24.690
our MBA for Executives colleagues

00:12:24.690 --> 00:12:25.930
the other day, and

00:12:25.930 --> 00:12:27.300
we were talking about content that

00:12:27.300 --> 00:12:28.650
was gonna go into their newsletter

00:12:28.650 --> 00:12:29.667
and they said,

00:12:29.667 --> 00:12:32.457
"What do you guys have in progress

00:12:32.457 --> 00:12:33.877
"or that you've recently published

00:12:33.877 --> 00:12:35.500
"that we can use in the newsletter?"

00:12:35.500 --> 00:12:37.040
and I said, "Oh give me a second",

00:12:37.040 --> 00:12:39.830
and I just clicked on
the EMBA Content label

00:12:39.830 --> 00:12:42.160
and it gives me every single thing that

00:12:42.160 --> 00:12:43.700
has been published, and it tells you

00:12:43.700 --> 00:12:45.270
exactly what status it's in.

00:12:45.270 --> 00:12:47.560
So, is it out for approval?

00:12:47.560 --> 00:12:49.240
Has it just been published?

00:12:49.240 --> 00:12:51.030
Have we promoted it on social?

00:12:51.030 --> 00:12:53.783
So, that's all really great.

00:12:53.783 --> 00:12:55.903
Happy to talk about that later.

00:12:57.276 --> 00:12:58.660
And at this point, I'm gonna

00:12:58.660 --> 00:13:00.390
turn things over to our colleague,

00:13:00.390 --> 00:13:01.700
Laurie, who's gonna talk a little bit

00:13:01.700 --> 00:13:03.283
about how we use Confluence.

00:13:07.620 --> 00:13:09.710
- So, I'm gonna talk about how

00:13:09.710 --> 00:13:11.870
and why we use Confluence.

00:13:11.870 --> 00:13:14.820
We discovered early on that

00:13:14.820 --> 00:13:16.820
Confluence is a great place for

00:13:16.820 --> 00:13:18.590
Content Planning

00:13:18.590 --> 00:13:21.560
and storing meeting notes and we also

00:13:21.560 --> 00:13:23.040
soon discovered it's a great place

00:13:23.040 --> 00:13:24.500
to collaborate.

00:13:24.500 --> 00:13:25.540
Within the past year,

00:13:25.540 --> 00:13:27.500
we started to rely on Confluence

00:13:27.500 --> 00:13:29.190
for content planning.

00:13:29.190 --> 00:13:30.220
It's and easy place for

00:13:30.220 --> 00:13:31.730
multiple people from teams

00:13:31.730 --> 00:13:33.540
across Yale SoM,

00:13:33.540 --> 00:13:36.470
to collaborate on one planning document.

00:13:36.470 --> 00:13:38.720
It also helps with Organization,

00:13:38.720 --> 00:13:39.820
because we can keep all

00:13:39.820 --> 00:13:41.270
the notes and discussions in

00:13:41.270 --> 00:13:44.220
one area that everyone can access

00:13:44.220 --> 00:13:45.860
instead of in someone's email

00:13:45.860 --> 00:13:48.000
which might get lost or forgotten,

00:13:48.000 --> 00:13:49.500
at least in my email (laughs).

00:13:50.530 --> 00:13:52.060
It also makes it easy to keep

00:13:52.060 --> 00:13:53.770
historical records.

00:13:53.770 --> 00:13:55.530
This helps if your area runs

00:13:55.530 --> 00:13:57.090
on cycles like ours,

00:13:57.090 --> 00:13:58.540
with a few key events

00:13:58.540 --> 00:14:00.860
like Commencement, or conferences

00:14:00.860 --> 00:14:02.740
that happen each year.

00:14:02.740 --> 00:14:04.815
By organizing and keeping our notes,

00:14:04.815 --> 00:14:06.760
we can look back on what we did

00:14:06.760 --> 00:14:08.120
the previous year,

00:14:08.120 --> 00:14:09.930
determine if it worked well,

00:14:09.930 --> 00:14:12.550
and then plan for the current year.

00:14:12.550 --> 00:14:14.280
Now, speaking of planning,

00:14:14.280 --> 00:14:16.410
we tend to do a lot of that.

00:14:16.410 --> 00:14:17.740
For social media, we use

00:14:17.740 --> 00:14:20.490
a planning document, which helps

00:14:20.490 --> 00:14:22.280
us track what's shareable, and

00:14:22.280 --> 00:14:23.960
when we plan to share it.

00:14:23.960 --> 00:14:26.090
Each Monday, our social team meets

00:14:26.090 --> 00:14:27.960
to discuss the week ahead.

00:14:27.960 --> 00:14:30.960
Since we use Hootsuite
to schedule content,

00:14:30.960 --> 00:14:33.080
we can decide what makes sense to post

00:14:33.080 --> 00:14:36.480
on Twitter, Facebook,
Instagram, and LinkedIn,

00:14:36.480 --> 00:14:38.250
and then schedule it.

00:14:38.250 --> 00:14:39.920
Once an item is scheduled,

00:14:39.920 --> 00:14:41.030
we can tick off the box

00:14:41.030 --> 00:14:42.723
to indicate that it's done.

00:14:43.760 --> 00:14:45.160
We also have a section where

00:14:45.160 --> 00:14:47.000
we keep track of items that are

00:14:47.000 --> 00:14:48.410
ready to share.

00:14:48.410 --> 00:14:51.440
It's been extremely
helpful to keep these lists

00:14:51.440 --> 00:14:53.646
to remind us of new stories, blogs,

00:14:53.646 --> 00:14:56.130
or even media mentions of faculty

00:14:56.130 --> 00:14:58.180
that we haven't shared yet.

00:14:58.180 --> 00:14:59.700
We have found that we are able to

00:14:59.700 --> 00:15:01.720
be more thoughtful on posts,

00:15:01.720 --> 00:15:04.100
by being proactive and planning,

00:15:04.100 --> 00:15:05.993
rather than being reactive.

00:15:06.960 --> 00:15:08.000
So, as I mentioned,

00:15:08.000 --> 00:15:09.380
we use Confluence a lot for

00:15:09.380 --> 00:15:10.870
editorial planning.

00:15:10.870 --> 00:15:14.080
From social, to newsletters,
to publications,

00:15:14.080 --> 00:15:15.920
it makes it easier to communicate

00:15:15.920 --> 00:15:18.390
and collaborate with multiple teams.

00:15:18.390 --> 00:15:20.480
It also helps increase the transparency

00:15:20.480 --> 00:15:22.520
of a project, and pretty much

00:15:22.520 --> 00:15:24.023
what our team is working on.

00:15:25.830 --> 00:15:27.900
Each month, the communications team

00:15:27.900 --> 00:15:30.400
helps send four individual newsletters

00:15:30.400 --> 00:15:32.530
for different admissions teams.

00:15:32.530 --> 00:15:35.510
Once we added this calendar to Confluence,

00:15:35.510 --> 00:15:37.180
it helped everyone realize that

00:15:37.180 --> 00:15:39.300
their project is just one of many

00:15:39.300 --> 00:15:41.820
that we're working on concurrently.

00:15:41.820 --> 00:15:43.750
It also helps us plan deadlines,

00:15:43.750 --> 00:15:45.780
and hopefully keeps the newsletters

00:15:45.780 --> 00:15:47.490
spaced apart, so that we're not

00:15:47.490 --> 00:15:49.440
in newsletter planning overload

00:15:49.440 --> 00:15:51.223
at any one time in the month.

00:15:54.500 --> 00:15:55.400
Whoopsie (laughs).

00:15:59.220 --> 00:16:02.570
So, this is our newsletter
planning document

00:16:02.570 --> 00:16:04.370
that Meredith referenced.

00:16:04.370 --> 00:16:06.660
So, when we meet with
the admissions teams,

00:16:06.660 --> 00:16:09.680
we use this document to plan the content

00:16:09.680 --> 00:16:11.120
for the month.

00:16:11.120 --> 00:16:12.180
By using a template,

00:16:12.180 --> 00:16:14.850
it helps organize the planning process.

00:16:14.850 --> 00:16:16.810
Once the content is laid out,

00:16:16.810 --> 00:16:19.410
we can track the progress of the stories.

00:16:19.410 --> 00:16:21.570
Many already have (mumbles) tickets,

00:16:21.570 --> 00:16:22.770
but anything that's unique

00:16:22.770 --> 00:16:23.990
content for the newsletter,

00:16:23.990 --> 00:16:26.100
we will create a (mumbles) ticket.

00:16:26.100 --> 00:16:27.440
Now we can keep track of when

00:16:27.440 --> 00:16:29.300
an item is submitted for review,

00:16:29.300 --> 00:16:30.180
and where it is in the

00:16:30.180 --> 00:16:31.880
approval process.

00:16:31.880 --> 00:16:33.490
In this example alone,

00:16:33.490 --> 00:16:35.590
in that last column of notes,

00:16:35.590 --> 00:16:37.590
you can see we have some items that are

00:16:37.590 --> 00:16:42.563
Published, Done, Editing, or In Progress.

00:16:44.353 --> 00:16:47.450
(laughs) I'm very heavy-handed in this.

00:16:47.450 --> 00:16:49.820
You can also prompt milestones,

00:16:49.820 --> 00:16:52.070
like we did for the newsletters.

00:16:52.070 --> 00:16:55.030
Once a check is performed,
we can tick the box.

00:16:55.030 --> 00:16:57.810
For newsletters, it serves as a reminder

00:16:57.810 --> 00:16:59.280
of the steps that need to be taken

00:16:59.280 --> 00:17:01.543
before the newsletter can be sent.

00:17:03.790 --> 00:17:04.760
So this is a look,

00:17:04.760 --> 00:17:05.593
just briefly,

00:17:05.593 --> 00:17:07.330
of our collaboration space.

00:17:07.330 --> 00:17:08.550
You can see we us it for

00:17:08.550 --> 00:17:11.920
Print Publications and Alumni Webinars,

00:17:11.920 --> 00:17:14.350
to Website Edits and Analytics.

00:17:14.350 --> 00:17:15.210
We chose to keep

00:17:15.210 --> 00:17:17.590
our collaborations in one project,

00:17:17.590 --> 00:17:18.670
but you can break them out

00:17:18.670 --> 00:17:19.980
into separate projects so that

00:17:19.980 --> 00:17:21.270
only a certain team

00:17:21.270 --> 00:17:23.210
can collaborate in that space

00:17:23.210 --> 00:17:24.673
if that works better for you.

00:17:25.510 --> 00:17:27.550
So, this has just been quick run-through

00:17:27.550 --> 00:17:29.540
of how and why we use Confluence.

00:17:29.540 --> 00:17:30.580
I am going to send it back

00:17:30.580 --> 00:17:31.983
to Reilly for some tips!

00:17:33.850 --> 00:17:34.920
- Thank you, Laurie.

00:17:34.920 --> 00:17:36.323
Make sure my mic is on.

00:17:37.518 --> 00:17:39.190
- (mumbles)

00:17:39.190 --> 00:17:40.890
- All right, thank you, Laurie.

00:17:40.890 --> 00:17:42.380
So, I'm gonna go ahead and

00:17:42.380 --> 00:17:43.630
wrap things up.

00:17:43.630 --> 00:17:45.200
We've taken a few bites

00:17:45.200 --> 00:17:47.020
out of this presentation.

00:17:47.020 --> 00:17:49.260
Sorry, I'm being a little cheesy.

00:17:49.260 --> 00:17:50.784
But, now I'm gonna go ahead and--

00:17:50.784 --> 00:17:51.847
(laughter)
Thank you.

00:17:51.847 --> 00:17:53.010
Get some advice for teams

00:17:53.010 --> 00:17:55.110
wanting to do the same.

00:17:55.110 --> 00:17:57.360
Wanna go in and use JIRA and Confluence

00:17:57.360 --> 00:17:59.320
for these non-technical uses, or

00:17:59.320 --> 00:18:01.330
really beyond IT.

00:18:01.330 --> 00:18:04.060
And the first and foremost piece

00:18:04.060 --> 00:18:05.490
of advice I can give you is

00:18:05.490 --> 00:18:06.970
to keep it simple, especially

00:18:06.970 --> 00:18:07.930
to start.

00:18:07.930 --> 00:18:10.070
And what that means, are those

00:18:10.070 --> 00:18:11.440
workflows that you saw

00:18:11.440 --> 00:18:12.930
that Meredith presented.

00:18:12.930 --> 00:18:14.150
That first one that said

00:18:14.150 --> 00:18:16.560
To Do, In Progress, Done,

00:18:16.560 --> 00:18:18.330
and that all of the statuses can

00:18:18.330 --> 00:18:20.460
transition to that status.

00:18:20.460 --> 00:18:22.440
That's what keeping it simple means.

00:18:22.440 --> 00:18:23.273
Get the folks

00:18:23.273 --> 00:18:24.730
who are going to be using this

00:18:25.950 --> 00:18:27.620
habituated to using the tool,

00:18:27.620 --> 00:18:29.640
with a simple workflow that's open.

00:18:29.640 --> 00:18:31.490
For those of you who speak JIRA,

00:18:31.490 --> 00:18:33.656
and I would also keep in mind,

00:18:33.656 --> 00:18:34.489
you know,

00:18:34.489 --> 00:18:36.490
think about their (mumbles) case.

00:18:36.490 --> 00:18:37.323
Think about how you're

00:18:37.323 --> 00:18:38.810
going to provide value for that

00:18:38.810 --> 00:18:41.160
person to use and to adopt

00:18:41.160 --> 00:18:42.460
Confluence and JIRA.

00:18:42.460 --> 00:18:43.710
How is it going to make their

00:18:43.710 --> 00:18:44.770
job easier?

00:18:44.770 --> 00:18:46.670
I think that's something that

00:18:46.670 --> 00:18:48.170
we learned along the way.

00:18:48.170 --> 00:18:49.800
Next, is of course,

00:18:49.800 --> 00:18:51.360
do the legwork.

00:18:51.360 --> 00:18:53.480
And this can mean many things.

00:18:53.480 --> 00:18:54.520
This could mean

00:18:54.520 --> 00:18:57.080
doing the legwork to get by and from

00:18:57.080 --> 00:18:58.690
leadership, and a great way

00:18:58.690 --> 00:19:02.140
to do that is go to Atlassian's website,

00:19:02.140 --> 00:19:06.050
sign-up for a trial, build a workflow,

00:19:06.050 --> 00:19:07.790
show what's possible.

00:19:07.790 --> 00:19:09.420
And then once you do that,

00:19:09.420 --> 00:19:10.253
instead of just,

00:19:10.253 --> 00:19:11.680
"Hey, we should do this",

00:19:11.680 --> 00:19:13.560
if you build it, and people really

00:19:13.560 --> 00:19:15.140
see what's possible,

00:19:15.140 --> 00:19:16.510
they kind of start to buy

00:19:16.510 --> 00:19:17.573
in a little bit more.

00:19:18.500 --> 00:19:21.360
And next, find champions,

00:19:21.360 --> 00:19:22.950
and this could be people from

00:19:22.950 --> 00:19:24.840
around the school,

00:19:24.840 --> 00:19:28.403
around the university,
outside the university.

00:19:29.300 --> 00:19:30.940
So, really anyone who's going to

00:19:30.940 --> 00:19:32.620
go ahead and jump into JIRA,

00:19:32.620 --> 00:19:34.300
jump into Confluence, and into these

00:19:34.300 --> 00:19:35.203
tools with you.

00:19:36.200 --> 00:19:38.780
And, I must say, you yourself,

00:19:38.780 --> 00:19:39.960
if you are driving adoption

00:19:39.960 --> 00:19:40.960
of this tool,

00:19:40.960 --> 00:19:43.070
you need to take that first jump.

00:19:43.070 --> 00:19:44.780
You need to be transparent.

00:19:44.780 --> 00:19:46.330
You need to...

00:19:46.330 --> 00:19:47.680
just go in

00:19:47.680 --> 00:19:49.010
and start using it.

00:19:49.010 --> 00:19:50.500
Start doing your work there

00:19:50.500 --> 00:19:52.420
and people will follow.

00:19:52.420 --> 00:19:53.390
Especially when they start

00:19:53.390 --> 00:19:55.290
to see some of the benefits that

00:19:55.290 --> 00:19:57.160
you might know and wanna communicate

00:19:57.160 --> 00:19:57.993
to your team.

00:19:59.500 --> 00:20:01.610
And, again, make sure it's

00:20:01.610 --> 00:20:02.560
the right solution for

00:20:02.560 --> 00:20:03.950
your team.

00:20:03.950 --> 00:20:05.570
This was great solution for us

00:20:05.570 --> 00:20:06.691
because, of course, these

00:20:06.691 --> 00:20:08.300
Atlassian tools have a large

00:20:08.300 --> 00:20:09.690
footprint already at

00:20:09.690 --> 00:20:11.160
the Yale School of Management.

00:20:11.160 --> 00:20:13.250
We're already using them.

00:20:13.250 --> 00:20:15.130
We have licenses, so hey.

00:20:15.130 --> 00:20:17.533
Why not use it for non-traditional

00:20:17.533 --> 00:20:19.670
or non-IT uses?

00:20:19.670 --> 00:20:21.863
Atlassian themselves actually do this.

00:20:23.080 --> 00:20:25.780
So, really, it just made sense,

00:20:25.780 --> 00:20:27.410
And it almost became like a

00:20:27.410 --> 00:20:29.540
lingua franca between

00:20:29.540 --> 00:20:32.750
our users, our folks in my department,

00:20:32.750 --> 00:20:35.990
the folks who support
us and our developers.

00:20:35.990 --> 00:20:37.260
We can all kind of speak this

00:20:37.260 --> 00:20:40.370
common language of, "Cheer up!".

00:20:40.370 --> 00:20:42.141
And, finally,

00:20:42.141 --> 00:20:43.490
remember that

00:20:43.490 --> 00:20:46.660
no tool is going to
solve all your problems.

00:20:46.660 --> 00:20:47.870
You know, the tool is only

00:20:47.870 --> 00:20:50.240
as strong as those underlying principles

00:20:50.240 --> 00:20:51.970
kind of, undergirding it.

00:20:51.970 --> 00:20:54.040
So, remember that this tool

00:20:54.040 --> 00:20:57.050
isn't to replace conversation

00:20:57.050 --> 00:20:58.460
face-to-face, or

00:20:58.460 --> 00:21:00.150
conversations or communication

00:21:00.150 --> 00:21:01.433
in any other manner.

00:21:02.670 --> 00:21:04.300
No matter how cool or flashy

00:21:04.300 --> 00:21:06.570
or what kind of automations you can do,

00:21:06.570 --> 00:21:08.490
again, keep it simple

00:21:08.490 --> 00:21:09.323
to start with.

00:21:09.323 --> 00:21:10.430
Don't overwhelm folks,

00:21:10.430 --> 00:21:11.750
including yourself, if you're driving

00:21:11.750 --> 00:21:12.960
adoption.

00:21:12.960 --> 00:21:14.760
And, again, really remember

00:21:14.760 --> 00:21:15.713
this last point.

00:21:16.890 --> 00:21:20.150
And with that, we are ready for questions,

00:21:20.150 --> 00:21:20.983
which I'm sure you might

00:21:20.983 --> 00:21:21.863
have a lot of!

00:21:24.650 --> 00:21:25.770
- And the first answer

00:21:25.770 --> 00:21:27.230
that is Handsome Dan in the

00:21:27.230 --> 00:21:28.730
Evans Hall Courtyard (laughs).

00:21:32.070 --> 00:21:34.903
(musical chimes)

